WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.524
A landing zone can take months to authorize. Your applications don't

00:00:04.524 --> 00:00:08.398
have to wait for it. Cloud Waypoint is a readiness program

00:00:08.398 --> 00:00:12.271
for missions adopting cloud through JWCC, so the mission arrives ready

00:00:12.271 --> 00:00:13.680
when the environment does.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:19.400
The readiness work runs alongside landing-zone delivery, not after it. Foundations

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:23.621
takes about eight weeks, the two advance weeks of the data

00:00:23.621 --> 00:00:27.841
call included. Dependency mapping runs about twelve, and launch preparation overlaps

00:00:27.841 --> 00:00:32.061
both. The whole shape is about twenty weeks. That timing is

00:00:32.061 --> 00:00:33.596
illustrative, not a promise.

00:00:35.096 --> 00:00:38.840
It starts with a three-hour working session on the mission, the

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:42.583
estate, the boundary, and current readiness. The workshop finds the right

00:00:42.583 --> 00:00:46.327
starting path. It does not choose your operating model. That decision

00:00:46.327 --> 00:00:47.689
stays with the mission.

00:00:49.189 --> 00:00:53.232
Foundations turns mission knowledge and existing artifacts into a proposed general

00:00:53.232 --> 00:00:57.274
support system: its boundary, its shared services, its security and identity

00:00:57.274 --> 00:00:59.480
layers, and an owner for each.

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:05.722
Dependency mapping shows how workloads actually communicate. Owners review what was

00:01:05.722 --> 00:01:10.465
seen and confirm what is needed. The result becomes migration waves

00:01:10.465 --> 00:01:15.208
and authorization inputs, built around application communities rather than isolated servers.

00:01:16.708 --> 00:01:20.919
A sound environment still fails without clear ownership. So day two

00:01:20.919 --> 00:01:25.131
is designed first: who monitors, who approves change, who responds to

00:01:25.131 --> 00:01:27.811
incidents, and who keeps the evidence current.

00:01:29.311 --> 00:01:33.256
The standards behind the method are public. The Reference Studio indexes

00:01:33.256 --> 00:01:37.202
the governing sources, from the DoD Cloud Computing Security Requirements Guide

00:01:37.202 --> 00:01:41.148
to the Risk Management Framework, each with its publisher, its edition,

00:01:41.148 --> 00:01:43.300
and when it was last verified.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:48.824
Search an impact level and every result says what its source

00:01:48.824 --> 00:01:52.848
supports, and what it doesn't. A listed service still needs its

00:01:52.848 --> 00:01:56.872
authorization-scope checks, and an impact-level label alone doesn't choose an environment.

00:01:58.372 --> 00:02:02.152
The GULF laboratory uses a fictional specimen to show how an

00:02:02.152 --> 00:02:05.933
authorization package comes together, and exactly where the model stops so

00:02:05.933 --> 00:02:07.652
a named official can decide.

00:02:09.152 --> 00:02:12.969
The method runs in a real application. This is the NatSec

00:02:12.969 --> 00:02:16.787
studio working a fictional engagement: one record, seven stages, and every

00:02:16.787 --> 00:02:18.870
fact traced back to its source.

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:24.344
Discovery feeds the applications register and the dependency map. The general

00:02:24.344 --> 00:02:28.318
support system is settled through nine decisions, each checked against its

00:02:28.318 --> 00:02:30.848
evidence and owned by a named person.

00:02:32.348 --> 00:02:36.374
Every deliverable prints from that same record: the executive brief, the

00:02:36.374 --> 00:02:40.401
transformation plan, the draft security plan. They arrive as packages, the

00:02:40.401 --> 00:02:44.427
midpoint snapshot at week four, then the readiness, transformation and final

00:02:44.427 --> 00:02:48.454
packages at week eight, each sized for its reader, each tracing

00:02:48.454 --> 00:02:50.284
back to the same evidence.

00:02:51.784 --> 00:02:56.524
Cloud Waypoint doesn't sell the cloud, replace your provider, or grant

00:02:56.524 --> 00:03:01.264
authorization. It builds the readiness between cloud access and mission adoption,

00:03:01.264 --> 00:03:03.418
alongside whoever delivers the environment.

00:03:04.918 --> 00:03:10.518
Evidence informs. Named individuals with authority decide, and the authorizing official

00:03:10.518 --> 00:03:16.118
keeps every authorization decision. To talk about your mission, visit cloud-waypoint

00:03:16.118 --> 00:03:17.136
dot com.
